ollow-up  monitoring and corrective treatment and  that's a loophole that desperately needs  to be plugged  you mentioned advertising there and I  know that the Advertising Standards  Authority have clamped down on a number  of ads that are trivializing and things  like boob jobs and  those jobs they've clamped down on that  but there needs to be some sort of  regulation on what seems to be a price  war as the industry grows surgeons seems  to be competing on price you do see  people traveling abroad for cheaper  treatment in Thailand for example  thousands of people from Australia the  United States England travel there for  cheaper surgery and also you get things  like I mean I get a lot of spam emails  from people like Groupon advertising  discounts for cosmetic surgery yes it's  it's very dispiriting to see that sort  of trivialization of what a potentially  life-changing decision for people and  that's one of many improvements to the  regulation of the industry that we want  to see another one really is about  empowering the patient now you have to  appreciate that some not all but some  people who are choosing to undergo  cosmetic treatment are themselves  vulnerable at that time they need to be  supported in making an informed decision  and one of the things that isn't present  in the private sector where the vast  majority of cosmetic treatment takes  place is a proper system of support and  independent advice for people if things  do go wrong in some places where it's  done badly it's a murky industry lacking  in transparency people don't know about  their rights they don't know how to  complain or to get safety concerns  looked into at a hospital or clinic  where they've been treated and they  haven't got that guarantee as an NHS  patient would have of independent help  and support and advice with their  complaint often people find it difficult  to find their way to us as a specialist  charity so all of this needs to be  addressed and if we empowered the  