sthetic surgeons but even a good  surgeon can go bad if something does go  wrong with a procedure like this there's  a certain stigma attached isn't it if  you've had cosmetic surgery it's not  life-threatening a lot of patients won't  know their rights will be too  embarrassed to follow it up and what  rights do they have Peter well they have  a right to make a complaint for example  all these organizations should be  regulated by the Care Quality Commission  and are required to have a complaints  procedure but private patients  unfortunately don't have the full rights  that an NHS patient would have for  example there's no final independent  arbitration in the sense of an ombudsman  which any of us who are NHS patients  would have and people aren't as  routinely to  told about places they can get  independent advice and support such as  my charity now that's where the  regulator's could be more robust in  insisting that people get information to  inform their decision whether or not to  have the surgery in the first place  so that they know about the risks and  they have a cooling-off period  before they actually undertake the  surgery but also when things go wrong  there needs to be a proper system of  redress and people need to be given  details of where they get advice and  information about their rights okay  Peter I'll leave the final word to Sarah  Sarah you've had a number of procedures  done and you're happy with the work  you've had done you've been very lucky  with the treatment you've had what would  your advice be to anyone sitting at home  thinking about having cosmetic surgery  well I mean plastic surgery actually  saved my life because if I didn't have  plastic surgery many years ago when I  was beaten to a pulp and my face was  rearranged for me I would have either  became a crazed alcoholic or I would  have topped myself and this is many of  the letters I receive with this content  in that because they can't get the  plastic surgery that they need